 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on",
   "text": "Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) happens because of changes in a gene called ARSA [Ed6d6d6d, Ee9410bd, E8e516a4, Ec8ffad9, E183315f, E56cfdf7, E2e68711, E7428e49, E38ac4d1]. This gene normally gives the body instructions to make an enzyme, which is a small helper that breaks things down, called arylsulfatase A [Ed6d6d6d]. When this enzyme does not work, the body cannot break down certain fatty substances the way it should [E2af53d8, E4a77853, E97bd66c, Ef63ba6a, E1919f8f, Ee547594]. These fatty substances then build up in the body, which is called glycosphingolipid storage [E7470be0, Ef9f74f4]. This buildup damages the coating around nerves, called myelin, in a process called demyelination [E623c955, Ed56cd1c, E6f580f8, E0f0023c, E5cf73aa, Eacb364c]. A paper reports that swelling and irritation in the nervous system, called neuroinflammation, may also play a part in MLD [E2511205].",
